  "query_preview": "..High pressure is centered east of the area this\nmorning, over Colorado and Wyoming. A Pacific Northwest trough is\nmaking its way over the top of the ridge, flattening that feature\nas it does so. For us, the primary impact is to add more of a\nwesterly component to the flow over Utah and southwest Wyoming,\nwhich is allowing some drier air to filter in. Satellite derived\nPWs are in the 0.8 to 1.0 inch range across the area. While this\nis still in the 75th to 90th percentile, this is a notable change\nfrom the near 99th percentile values observed over southern Utah\nyesterday, and this gradual drying trend will continue through the\nremainder of the weekend. Thus, while afternoon and evening\nconvection is still anticipated, primarily focused over the higher\nterrain, coverage will be a bit less, and there is a bit more\nsteering flow, which slightly decreases the potential for\nflooding. \n\nDespite the drying, enough moisture will be around to keep low\ntemperatures elevated. In the valleys, this means that many valley \nlocations will stay in the 70s to around 80 overnight with high\ntemperatures rising above 100F, with the lack of relief\ncontributing to an increased risk for heat related illness. Main \nchange with this forecast package was to add Heat Advisories for \nZion National Park, south central Utah, and western Canyonlands, \nas well as to add an Extreme Heat Warning for Lake Powell, all in \neffect through Monday like the existing heat headlines for \nnorthwest Utah. \n\nBy Tuesday, shifting of the ridge is expected to return Utah and\nsouthwest Wyoming to a more southerly flow aloft, which would draw\nmonsoon moisture back northward into the area, with increasing\nconfidence that this moisture would then remain in place for most\nof the remainder of the upcoming week. It looks like that the\nincreasing moisture may moderate high temperatures somewhat for\nTuesday into Wednesday, though low temperatures will most likely\nremain elevated. This should decrease the HeatRisk a bit, but\nthere are some indications temperatures will be on the rise again\nlate in the upcoming week, something to monitor.",
